{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And every terumah, etc. — to him it shall belong, etc.</b> One needs to know why the verse repeated one matter three times: <b>and every terumah, etc. — to him it shall belong</b>, <b>and every man his holy things, etc.</b>, <b>every man who gives, etc.</b> Further, why did it repeat three times <b>to him it shall belong</b>, and our Rabbis of blessed memory in the Sifrei explained: ״לוֹ יִהְיֶה״ comes to assign the first fruits, which are called רֵאשִׁית כַּתְּרוּמָה, brought to the House of HaShem, so that they shall belong to the priest given to him. ״לוֹ יִהְיוּ״ stated in the second verse is to say that if the priest offered his holy things even in a watch that is not his, they are his. And they said ״וַאֲשֶׁר יִתֵּן לַכֹּהֵן לוֹ יִהְיֶה״ — if one gave him the redemption of his firstborn son and he died after thirty days, it belongs to the priest and he shall not return it. And there are many derashot like these, but we need to settle the verse according to its plain sense." "It appears to explain the three expressions that come as one: they teach the totality of twenty-four gifts that HaShem apportioned to the priests. The Rambam wrote them in chapter 1 of Hilkhot Bikkurim, and this is his language: the twenty-four gifts given to the priests are divided into five parts, which are three. The first are consecrated items that they acquire from the Sanctuary, and these are: the flesh of a sin offering, the flesh of a guilt offering, communal peace offerings, the remainder of the omer, the leftovers of meal offerings, the two loaves, the showbread, the oil of a leper, the hides of consecrated animals, the breast and thigh of peace offerings, the portion separated from the thanksgiving offering, the portion separated from the ram of a nazirite, the firstborn of a pure animal, and the first fruits. These are fourteen gifts, all of which the owners must bring and give to the priest, and all are sacred. The second division are terumah, and terumat ma'aser, and the challah. And the third are the gifts, and the first of the fleece, an inherited field, the redemption of the son, the redemption of a firstborn donkey, the theft of a convert, and devoted property — these are unconsecrated and the priest must pursue after them. End quote." "<b>And these</b> are the meaning of the verses: corresponding to the first division, which comprises fourteen gifts that have two characteristics — they are sacred and Israel must bring them and give them to the priest — He said <b>and every terumah of all the holy things of the children of Israel</b>: He has mentioned over them the name of holiness. <b>that they offer to HaShem</b> — He has mentioned their bringing, that they offer them to the consecrated place. And corresponding to the second division, which are terumah and terumat ma'aser and challah, He said <b>and every man his holy things shall be his</b> — He used the language of consecration, to say that it speaks of consecrated gifts, and did not mention offering them because the priest pursues after them. And corresponding to the seven gifts He said <b>every man who gives to the priest</b> — He did not mention a name of holiness, since they are entirely unconsecrated, and He was precise to say <b>who gives</b>, for the priest has no claim in them except from the moment of giving, according to the law of gifts, until they reach the hand of the recipient. And see what I have written in Hilkhot Matnot Kehunnah (Pri To'ar, siman 61). And He said <b>to him it shall belong</b> — that the priest cannot refuse to take them, as stated in their words (Yoreh De'ah 246:4). And from now on there is a sound reason in the verse's dividing them into three categories, saying over each one of the three divisions ״לוֹ יִהְיֶה״ — that he must take everything that comes to him from them, which would not have been understood had He said it over only one division alone."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {2 "The citation 'Yoreh De'ah 246:4' corresponds to the Or HaChaim's reference יורה דעה ש״ו, ד, where ש״ו = 246 and ד = 4 in gimatriya/letter notation. 'Pri To'ar' (פרי תואר) is the Or HaChaim's own halakhic work; the abbreviation is resolved by inference."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:06:25.972450307Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}